                <pb facs="0652" n="556" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg556"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">715.1715/209: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schuyler</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-27T14:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 27,
                                1921—2 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>26. A cable from the Chargé in Tegucigalpa states it to be the general opinion
                    there that the revolutionary movement in Nicaragua is being backed by the
                    Salvadorian Government, with the assistance of Honduranean <hi
                        rendition="#italic">emigrados</hi>, with a view to defeating the Central
                    American Union. The Nicaraguan Minister in Washington has expressed an opinion
                    to the same effect. Please inform the Department whether, in your opinion, there
                    is any ground for this belief.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
